.TH PTYFS 4
.SH NAME
ptyfs \- pseudo-terminals for APE
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /bin/ape/ptyfs
[
.B -D
] [
.B -s
.I srvname
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ptyfs
serves pseudo-terminals: a terminal emulator holds one side, the
master, and the programs in the terminal the other, the slave.
APE's
.IR posix_openpt
(and
.IR _getpty )
starts it when
.B /dev/ptyclone
is missing; it posts
.BI /srv/ srvname
(default
.BR ptyfs )
and the caller mounts that after
.BR /dev .
.PP
Opening
.B ptyclone
makes a pty
.IR n :
the file descriptor is its master
.BI ttym n\f1;
the slave is
.BI ptty n
.RI ( ptsname
names it),
.BI pconsctl n
is the slave's
.IR consctl ,
and
.BI ptyctl n
holds the terminal's modes.
What the master writes is typed at the terminal, what it reads is the
terminal's output.
The pty's files go when no side holds them open.
.PP
A POSIX program makes the slave its terminal by
.I setsid
(a namespace and note group of its own), opening the slave and
.B ioctl(fd, TIOCSCTTY, 0):
APE binds
.BI ptty n
on
.B /dev/cons
and
.BI pconsctl n
on
.BR /dev/consctl ,
so native programs run in it as in a
.IR rio (1)
window; the note group is the terminal's.
.SS Line discipline
In canonical mode
.RB ( ICANON )
input is edited by lines: the erase character (DEL, or backspace)
erases a character (a UTF-8 rune), the kill character (control-U) the
line, control-W (with
.BR IEXTEN )
a word; the end-of-file character (control-D) ends a read, an empty one
at the start of a line.
A read returns one line at most.
Input is echoed
.RB ( ECHO ,
erase as backspace, space, backspace with
.BR ECHOE );
carriage return becomes newline
.RB ( ICRNL ).
With
.BR ISIG ,
control-C posts the note
.B interrupt
to the terminal's note group and control-\e
.BR quit ;
control-Z is dropped (no job control).
Output's newline becomes carriage return, newline
.RB ( OPOST ,
.BR ONLCR ).
Raw mode
.RB ( ICANON
off) passes bytes as they come;
.B VMIN
0 makes a read return at once.
Writing
.B rawon
to the slave's
.I consctl
turns
.B ICANON
and
.B ECHO
off until
.B rawoff
or until that file is closed, as
.IR cons (3)
does.
.PP
When the master's last descriptor closes, the note group gets
.B hangup
and the slave reads end of file; writes fail.
When the slave was open and no longer is, the master reads end of file.
.SS Control
Each pty has a control file
.BI ptyctl n
(APE's
.IR tcgetattr ,
.IR tcsetattr ,
.IR tcsetpgrp ,
.IR ioctl ):
reading gives
.IP
.B IOR
.I iflag oflag cflag lflag
.IR cc ...
.I pgrp rows cols xpixels ypixels
.PP
(flags and control characters in hex, with the values of APE's
.BR termios.h );
writing
.B IOW
.I iflag oflag cflag lflag
.IR cc ...
sets them,
.B IOW note
.I pid
the note group (that of
.IR pid ),
.B IOW winsize
.I rows cols xpixels ypixels
the size
.RB ( TIOCSWINSZ ,
.BR TIOCGWINSZ ).
.PP
The
.B -D
flag prints the 9P messages.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/ape/cmd/ptyfs
.br
.B /sys/src/ape/lib/bsd/pty.c
.br
.B /sys/src/ape/lib/bsd/ioctl.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cons (3),
.IR rio (4),
.IR vt (1)
.SH BUGS
A changed size posts no note
.RB ( SIGWINCH ):
a native program would die of it; programs ask with
.BR TIOCGWINSZ .
.B VTIME
is ignored, and so is
.B VMIN
above 1.
Flow control
.RB ( IXON )
is not done.
